1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,360
Well, good morning, church.

2
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:14,960
Thanks so much for hanging out with us on this holiday weekend.

3
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:20,360
I know for many of you, it was a sacrifice to get here, and it was a sacrifice worth

4
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:22,400
making for the Lord.

5
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:27,300
I am personally thankful for our country, thankful for the people who have worked hard

6
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:30,380
and sacrificed to maintain it and to keep it safe.

7
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:37,500
However, the older I get and the more I follow Jesus, the more I realize that it is primary

8
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:44,720
for the follower of Jesus to put their allegiance in the kingdom of God first over any earthly

9
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:45,100
nation.

10
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:51,260
And so I celebrate today and thankful for the country that God has given us, but even more

11
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:55,800
thankful for the kingdom of God that he is building through his church, and that he will return

12
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:58,580
to set up once and for all, wipe away every tear from our eyes.

13
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:02,880
There will be no more mourning and no more pain, no more war sacrifice.

14
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:06,460
So that's what our hope is in this morning.

15
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:14,760
And the church, the big C church and local congregations and Sunday morning services are set up to

16
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:21,680
remind us of these things in the midst of a broken world, a fallen world, where we gather together

17
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:27,520
as believers to fellowship, to sing truth together, to hear from God's word together, to break bread

18
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:31,500
together, we do all of those things to remind us of these truths.

19
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:40,140
However, in the American church, if I'm being honest, me personally, we tend to elevate the sermon, the

20
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:43,740
individual monologue, above some of the other elements.

21
00:01:43,740 --> 00:01:49,440
And that was no more apparent to me than this week, yesterday morning, when I got a text from

22
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:55,300
Dol Pazmino, a brother in Christ who I love and am thankful for, telling me that he had COVID.

23
00:01:55,300 --> 00:02:02,240
And instead of my primary concern being for him, my primary concern, confessing sin to you, was that you guys

24
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:04,240
might have to listen to a bad sermon today.

25
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:08,100
And I think that reveals a couple things about my heart.

26
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:15,720
One, I think that reveals some identity issues that I have to work through, where I feel like I need to be seen as a

27
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:17,460
halfway decent communicator, right?

28
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:22,720
Like, I don't want to come up here and be bad at communicating, but also that reveals an identity issue in my heart.

29
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:31,500
It also reveals to me, at least personally, that I have an overemphasis of the 30-minute monologue on Sunday morning to the

30
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:33,220
importance of the health of the overall church.

31
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:40,420
Because the gates of hell are not going to prevail against God's church, whether I preach a bad sermon on a Sunday or not.

32
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:48,180
So, all that being said, I did spend a significant amount of time yesterday with the Lord and my computer.

33
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:52,880
And I do believe that I have some encouraging things for us this morning.

34
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:55,960
But it's not polished.

35
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:58,320
It's probably not the greatest thing that you've ever heard.

36
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:01,920
And I think that that's a good thing, that we can learn this morning.

37
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:08,420
That the Sunday morning monologue is not the primary thing that God wants to use to build his church.

38
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:20,760
It's individual believers gathering together as a body using the gifts that he has given them through his Holy Spirit to be conformed to the image of Christ and to do the work of the kingdom in our city.

39
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:22,500
So, let's pray.

40
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:26,140
I will give what I hope to be an encouraging word.

41
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:30,000
And ultimately, God is going to get glory through whatever happens this morning.

42
00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:33,740
Father, we are so thankful for the church.

43
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:42,420
We are thankful for the body of believers that you have gathered together here at Canvas and in local churches all over the city of Winchester.

44
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:44,520
That are going to preach the gospel.

45
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:49,520
That are going to use the gifts to serve one another in the community.

46
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:52,040
That are going to preach your truth.

47
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:53,600
Sing your truth.

48
00:03:54,100 --> 00:04:02,360
That are going to eat your body broken for us and drink your blood shed for us to remind us that there is a hope far greater than the nation of America.

49
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:07,660
God, may you get glory through this service.

50
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:10,620
May you conform us into your image for your glory.

51
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:12,220
In Jesus' name I pray.

52
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:12,920
Amen.

53
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:28,720
I don't know if you feel this, but I feel that oftentimes the culture that I live in is dictated by this five-inch brick of, you know, whatever materials this phone is made out of.

54
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:33,780
I would say iPhone, but I know some of you people out there don't know what's good and you have an Android.

55
00:04:33,780 --> 00:04:35,080
That's okay.

56
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:36,500
Just kidding.

57
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:47,580
So much of our culture is dictated by the information, the content that we consume on a daily basis, and I find myself grabbing this phone so often first thing in the morning.

58
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:54,560
A couple weeks ago, I remember first thing in the morning grabbing my phone, and it's super emotionally healthy to check your email at 6.30 in the morning.

59
00:04:54,560 --> 00:05:00,120
Just in case anybody was wondering, it's a good thing to do for your soul to check your email at 6.30 in the morning.

60
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:10,100
And so I was checking my email in bed at 6.30 in the morning, and I got an email notification from this thing I signed up for years ago called 50-50 Friday.

61
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:12,620
This gives you a bunch of deals, right?

62
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:14,180
Deals on deals.

63
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:23,540
And I haven't used it in multiple years, but I opened up my email, and there was a deal for a golf outing, which you guys have found out by now that I like golf.

64
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:31,160
And so it was like three rounds of golf for like $25, but there was like a minimum offer of the amount that they could have.

65
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:32,300
And so I was like, I got to get it.

66
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:33,040
Click on it.

67
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:34,020
Go to pay for it.

68
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:35,120
And like I can see them.

69
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:35,960
It's like got a timer.

70
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:38,440
There was 25 when I opened it, and there was like 21.

71
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:40,120
I'm like trying to put in my information.

72
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:43,060
I'm like, if I don't get this cheap golf, I'm going to be mad, right?

73
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:45,760
This is the thing that's happening in my heart at 6.30 in the morning.

74
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:47,980
I go to pay, and I'm like, perfect.

75
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:48,920
I'm going to use Apple Pay.

76
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:50,420
It'll take me 10 seconds, and it's done.

77
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:54,460
Guess who forgot to set up Apple Pay when he was setting up his new iPhone?

78
00:05:54,460 --> 00:05:56,620
Marissa's thought.

79
00:05:56,620 --> 00:05:57,320
It's Marissa's thought.

80
00:05:57,320 --> 00:06:03,680
I forgot to set up Apple Pay, so then I'm mad that Apple Pay is not like immediately.

81
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:04,540
And I'm like, that's fine.

82
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:06,060
I probably got my credit card saved, right?

83
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:07,600
So I auto-fill the credit card.

84
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:08,040
Guess what?

85
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:09,080
The credit card's out of date.

86
00:06:09,080 --> 00:06:12,800
And like meanwhile, the things are just ticking down and ticking down and ticking down.

87
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:16,700
I'm like, I'm not going to get the cheap golf because I was too lazy to set up Apple Pay,

88
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:22,560
and now all of this is happening at once, and I was frustrated in my bed at 6.30 in the morning.

89
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:22,920
Why?

90
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:26,420
Because I live in a culture of instant gratification.

91
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:31,820
I live in a culture where I can pretty much get what I want the moment that I want to get it.

92
00:06:31,820 --> 00:06:35,780
And then I moved from, I finally got it.

93
00:06:35,780 --> 00:06:36,160
Don't worry.

94
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:37,900
I got the cheap golf.

95
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:39,400
It should be in the mail any time.

96
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:43,740
But I'm annoyed that I don't have the instant gratification that I want.

97
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:52,520
And then I open up my Twitter account to find out that the pastor that I look up to and admire had some sexual misconduct allegations.

98
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:59,260
And that day, every sermon that he had ever preached had been wiped from the Internet.

99
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:04,400
Nothing that he has ever said about Jesus exists anymore on the Internet.

100
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:10,360
Because not only do we live in a culture of instant gratification, we live in a cancel culture where if you do one thing wrong,

101
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:14,260
if you step on the wrong, I'm not downplaying sexual misconduct.

102
00:07:14,260 --> 00:07:16,320
I'm saying he didn't even have time for a trial.

103
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:17,880
He didn't have time for allegations.

104
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:19,540
It was everything was gone.

105
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:22,660
You guys felt cancel culture at all?

106
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:23,000
Anybody?

107
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,080
Felt like you say the one wrong thing and you're gone forever.

108
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:27,940
There's no grace.

109
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:32,620
We live in a culture of instant gratification and a cancel culture.

110
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:43,680
And I think, as I studied patience, Christian patience, yesterday, I realized that it might be a difficult culture for us to create the fruit,

111
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:46,880
for the Holy Spirit to create the fruit of patience in our lives.

112
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:50,160
Because ultimately, patience is about two main things.

113
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:57,240
Christian patience is about extending grace to difficult people and about having endurance in difficult circumstances.

114
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:06,340
And if patience is about extending grace to difficult people, that flies in the face of cancel culture that says the moment somebody steps on your toes,

115
00:08:06,340 --> 00:08:11,400
the moment somebody sends you or says the wrong thing the wrong way, you can cut them out of your lives forever.

116
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:17,120
Or a culture of instant gratification where we get everything that we want the moment that we want it.

117
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:31,180
So, no wonder Paul says that we need the Holy Spirit to produce patience in our life.

118
00:08:31,180 --> 00:08:36,740
We live in a life where it's not naturally created in our hearts and minds.

119
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:48,760
So, we're dependent on the Holy Spirit to produce this kind of patience in our life that allows us to extend grace to difficult people and to have endurance in difficult circumstances.

120
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:56,140
So, if you have your Bible, we're going to kind of have a jumping off passage in James chapter 5, and we'll read a bunch of Scripture.

121
00:08:56,980 --> 00:09:00,460
But I really just want to unpack those two ideas of patience.

122
00:09:00,460 --> 00:09:09,200
I think that Christian patience really is about sending grace to difficult people and about enduring difficult circumstances.

123
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:14,020
We, if you've been around campus for a while, you know we preach through the book of James not too long ago.

124
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:18,020
And it's funny, this passage about patience was one that we kind of tacked on to the end of the message.

125
00:09:18,100 --> 00:09:23,080
We really didn't get much time to unpack it, so I'm thankful that we have the time now.

126
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:26,660
So, James chapter 5, starting in verse 7, it says,

127
00:09:26,660 --> 00:09:34,300
Dear brothers and sisters, be patient, be patient as you wait for the Lord's return.

128
00:09:34,300 --> 00:09:40,540
Consider the farmer who patiently waits for the rain in the fall and in the spring.

129
00:09:40,540 --> 00:09:44,660
They eagerly look for the valuable harvest to ripen.

130
00:09:44,660 --> 00:09:46,460
You too must be patient.

131
00:09:47,440 --> 00:09:50,420
Take courage, for the coming of the Lord is near.

132
00:09:50,420 --> 00:09:51,840
And then he moves on to his application.

133
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:57,000
If you're going to be a patient person, if you are going to have patience in your life, what does it look like?

134
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:02,800
Verse 9, don't grumble about each other, brothers and sisters, or you will be judged.

135
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:06,220
For look, the judge is standing at the door.

136
00:10:06,220 --> 00:10:12,380
So I think the first aspect of Christian patience that James says is that if you're going to have patience,

137
00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:14,620
you've got to have grace for difficult people.

138
00:10:14,620 --> 00:10:17,120
He says be a people of patience.

139
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:19,320
Allow the Lord to produce patience in your life.

140
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:24,540
And if you're going to be a patient person, don't grumble with your brothers and sisters who are difficult.

141
00:10:24,540 --> 00:10:30,560
See, James knows that we will regularly encounter difficult people in our lives.

142
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:40,860
Or people that aren't usually difficult in our lives that we love and are thankful for, for whatever reason, become difficult when they're tired or hungry or grumpy or any of those things.

143
00:10:40,860 --> 00:10:41,040
Right?

144
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:46,320
James knows that there's an enemy to patience, which is other people.

145
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:48,640
Anybody feel that?

146
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:52,800
Anybody feel that the enemy to your patience is the people in your life?

147
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:54,080
I feel that.

148
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:54,660
Patience.

149
00:10:54,660 --> 00:10:58,720
And patience is usually easier with people that we like.

150
00:10:58,720 --> 00:10:59,320
Right?

151
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:00,880
Like there's a natural.

152
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:03,040
So I think sometimes we give ourselves credit.

153
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:06,760
We call ourselves patient people because we're patient with the people that we like.

154
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:08,220
That doesn't count.

155
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:19,840
I love how I'm reading this book called Nice by Sharon Miller, and she puts it this way, that niceness or friendship, relationship, makes it all the more likely that we will forgive misbehavior or deny it altogether.

156
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:23,980
So if you're in here and you're patient with people that you love, I'm happy for you.

157
00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:25,340
That doesn't count.

158
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:28,040
It counts a little bit, but not all the way.

159
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:28,700
Right?

160
00:11:28,700 --> 00:11:39,100
And it's also easier for us to have patience with difficult people when we're well-rested and when we're in a healthy emotional and physical state.

161
00:11:39,100 --> 00:11:40,860
Would you agree with me?

162
00:11:40,860 --> 00:11:44,740
It's easier to have patience when you're in a good head space, when you're in a good emotional space.

163
00:11:44,740 --> 00:11:52,720
And one of the reasons I can know this and believe this is because I have spent a lot of time with many of you and your children.

164
00:11:53,740 --> 00:12:01,180
For instance, Michelle oftentimes brings Meadow and sometimes Micah into the office while we work together in one space.

165
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:05,320
And I have a lot of patience for Meadow and Micah.

166
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:07,360
You know why?

167
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:09,660
I don't live with them.

168
00:12:09,660 --> 00:12:16,360
I wasn't up until 3 o'clock in the morning with a crying baby who puked all over me.

169
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:20,160
I wasn't there at 5 a.m. when they came in with the ear infection.

170
00:12:20,340 --> 00:12:27,020
Like, I don't have kids yet, and so I wake up pretty much almost always in a healthy emotional and physical state.

171
00:12:27,020 --> 00:12:36,780
And so I can go over to the Smith's house at 6 p.m. when they've been up with the kids since 5 a.m.,

172
00:12:36,780 --> 00:12:40,580
gone to work, picked them up from school, got there, and the kids are running around like a mess.

173
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:46,780
And I can have an hour's worth of patience towards their children because I didn't have to deal with any of it.

174
00:12:46,840 --> 00:12:48,800
And I get to go home at the end of the night.

175
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:56,400
But the type of patience that James is asking us to have is not the easy kind that we can have when we're emotionally or physically healthy

176
00:12:56,400 --> 00:12:57,900
or with people that we like.

177
00:12:57,900 --> 00:13:02,740
He's asking us to have the same patience that Jesus Christ had to you and I.

178
00:13:03,140 --> 00:13:09,780
When we were the most difficult of people, Jesus was patient with us.

179
00:13:09,780 --> 00:13:18,140
Christ, Christian patience, the kind that the fruit of the Spirit requires, is the kind of patience that is extended even to the worst people

180
00:13:18,140 --> 00:13:21,460
in the most difficult circumstances.

181
00:13:21,460 --> 00:13:25,760
This is the type of patience that the Holy Spirit wants to produce in you and I.

182
00:13:25,880 --> 00:13:31,020
Anybody can have patience for people they like when they're in a healthy emotional and physical state.

183
00:13:31,020 --> 00:13:37,200
It's the type of patience that is extended to the most difficult people in the worst of times.

184
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:43,060
Now, disclaimer, I've heard this same argument made.

185
00:13:43,060 --> 00:13:49,660
Preachers have encouraged people to stay in unhealthy situations for the sake of patience.

186
00:13:49,660 --> 00:13:55,200
Not telling you to put yourself in dangerous situations and remain in dangerous situations.

187
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:58,840
You can have patience while getting yourself into a safe place.

188
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:05,120
But this kind of patience that Christians are called to, I think if we're honest,

189
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:08,840
we're far more eager to receive it than we are to extend it to others.

190
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:16,900
Think about patience, we think about the unforgiving servant in Matthew 18, this parable that Jesus teaches.

191
00:14:16,900 --> 00:14:21,940
It's got a greater meaning, but I think there's an application for patience here.

192
00:14:21,940 --> 00:14:23,580
He says this in Matthew 18,

193
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:30,960
The kingdom of heaven can be compared to a king who decided to bring his accounts up to date with his servants,

194
00:14:30,960 --> 00:14:32,380
who had borrowed money from him.

195
00:14:32,380 --> 00:14:38,280
In the process, one of his debtors was brought in who owed millions of dollars.

196
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:43,760
As I've heard some scholars say, it could be up to a billion dollars that he could not pay.

197
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:51,860
So his master ordered that he be sold along with his wife and his children and everything he owed to pay the debt.

198
00:14:51,860 --> 00:14:56,300
But the man fell down before his master and begged him, please be what?

199
00:14:56,300 --> 00:14:59,920
Be patient with me and I will pay it all.

200
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:01,260
All I need is a little bit of time.

201
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:05,320
You know, I only have like a few dollars, but give me a couple months and I will have millions.

202
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:07,340
I promise you, I can do it.

203
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:09,280
Be patient with me and I will pay it all.

204
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:13,580
Then this master was filled with pity for him and released him.

205
00:15:13,580 --> 00:15:17,400
And not only did he extend patience to him, he extended grace to him.

206
00:15:17,400 --> 00:15:18,880
He forgave his debt.

207
00:15:18,880 --> 00:15:20,100
Unmerited kindness.

208
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:27,540
But when the man left the king, he went to a fellow servant who owed him a few thousand dollars.

209
00:15:27,540 --> 00:15:32,180
He grabbed him by the throat and demanded instant payment.

210
00:15:32,180 --> 00:15:37,100
See, the man was asking for patience, but he didn't need patience.

211
00:15:37,100 --> 00:15:37,980
He needed a miracle.

212
00:15:37,980 --> 00:15:41,580
This was an unforgivable amount of debt.

213
00:15:41,580 --> 00:15:43,940
And he was thinking, you know what?

214
00:15:43,980 --> 00:15:45,180
All I need is a little bit of patience.

215
00:15:45,180 --> 00:15:49,800
He painted his situation in a far less difficult light.

216
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:51,320
Any of us ever do that with God?

217
00:15:51,320 --> 00:15:56,160
God, I just, you know, you forgive me a little bit, but I'm a pretty good person.

218
00:15:56,160 --> 00:15:58,660
I do a lot of good things.

219
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:00,360
I just need a little bit of patience.

220
00:16:00,360 --> 00:16:09,380
See, I think sometimes we don't have patience for other people because we are far underqualified to realize how patient God has been with us.

221
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:18,740
We are in millions of dollars of debt to the Lord, and we are going into his presence saying, I just need a little patience, just a little forgiveness.

222
00:16:18,740 --> 00:16:19,600
I haven't been that bad.

223
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:20,780
I haven't done that many things.

224
00:16:20,780 --> 00:16:23,740
I haven't fallen that short of your standard.

225
00:16:23,740 --> 00:16:36,000
And if that is our perspective about our relationship between us and God, no wonder we have less patience for the people that sin against us because we far underestimate how much we have sinned against God.

226
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:40,960
We are so eager to receive the patience of the Lord and so stingy to extend it.

227
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:49,940
Once he received not just patience but grace, he went out and immediately treated his fellow servant with zero patience for grace.

228
00:16:49,940 --> 00:16:57,320
How often do we lack patience with difficult people because in that moment we forget how patient God has been to us.

229
00:16:58,760 --> 00:17:05,220
One of our, many of our favorite characters in the Bible, the Apostle Paul, wrote most of the New Testament.

230
00:17:05,220 --> 00:17:13,380
God's saving the Apostle Paul was a demonstration for all of us for the rest of time to understand how patient God is.

231
00:17:13,380 --> 00:17:15,040
It says this in 1 Timothy.

232
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:16,220
Paul says,

233
00:17:16,220 --> 00:17:19,780
This is a trustworthy saying and everyone should accept it.

234
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:25,860
Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners and I am the worst of them all.

235
00:17:25,860 --> 00:17:28,880
But God have mercy on me.

236
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:29,260
Why?

237
00:17:29,260 --> 00:17:38,260
So that Christ Jesus could use me, Paul, as a prime example of his great patience with even the worst sinners.

238
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:44,740
So that others will realize that they too can believe in him and receive eternal life.

239
00:17:44,740 --> 00:17:45,320
Paul says,

240
00:17:45,320 --> 00:17:47,280
I murdered Christians.

241
00:17:47,280 --> 00:17:57,540
Literally, I was the prime enemy of the kingdom of God in Rome right after Jesus was crucified and resurrected.

242
00:17:57,540 --> 00:18:01,980
I was doing the absolute worst possible things I could do in the face of God.

243
00:18:02,060 --> 00:18:06,900
And he saved me, not just to save me, but to show you how patient that he is.

244
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:14,240
And I think oftentimes we lose sight of how patient God is.

245
00:18:14,240 --> 00:18:21,860
We can extend patience to difficult people because God has been far more patient with us when we were far more difficult towards him.

246
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:25,780
The character of God is patient.

247
00:18:27,080 --> 00:18:33,440
This is what God says about himself to Moses in Exodus 34, verses 6 and 7.

248
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:35,580
God is showing himself.

249
00:18:35,580 --> 00:18:38,040
You guys remember the story, right, where Moses wants to see God.

250
00:18:38,040 --> 00:18:42,260
And God's like, yeah, that's not going to work out because you'll just evaporate.

251
00:18:42,260 --> 00:18:49,100
But I'll stick you in this cave and you face backwards and I'll like breeze past you and it'll be awesome and you'll get some of me.

252
00:18:49,100 --> 00:18:50,560
And this is the story.

253
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:52,760
And God's breezing past Moses in the cave.

254
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:55,160
And this is what he tells Moses is true about himself.

255
00:18:55,160 --> 00:19:12,020
He says, the Lord, the Lord, compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, God is patient, abounding in love and faithfulness, maintaining love to thousands and forgiving wickedness, rebellion, and sin.

256
00:19:12,020 --> 00:19:14,180
This is what God says is true about himself.

257
00:19:14,180 --> 00:19:18,300
He is patient, slow to anger, abounding in love towards difficult people.

258
00:19:20,560 --> 00:19:26,380
And just in case you attempted to kind of not believe how patient God is, read the Old Testament.

259
00:19:26,380 --> 00:19:36,180
The Old Testament is God proving that what he said to Moses about himself is true generation after generation of the nation of Israel turning their back on him.

260
00:19:36,180 --> 00:19:37,540
Just read the book of Judges.

261
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:44,340
The book of Judges is chapter after chapter saying that God's people whom he called out, delivered from slavery, said, you know what?

262
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:46,180
We're just going to do things the way that we want to do.

263
00:19:46,180 --> 00:19:48,200
And then it doesn't work.

264
00:19:48,200 --> 00:19:49,420
And their lives are ruined.

265
00:19:49,420 --> 00:19:52,740
And a new person comes and becomes in charge and says, you know what?

266
00:19:52,740 --> 00:19:57,960
Let's just keep doing things the way that we're doing it and keep rebelling against God.

267
00:19:58,040 --> 00:20:04,080
And over and over and over again, God delivers them from their wicked rebellion.

268
00:20:04,080 --> 00:20:12,220
They're pushed back into slavery and he redeems them back, gives them his law again, over and over and over again.

269
00:20:12,220 --> 00:20:14,540
God is a patient God.

270
00:20:14,540 --> 00:20:18,740
God is patient to the most difficult people in the worst circumstances.

271
00:20:18,740 --> 00:20:22,000
And this is the type of patience that he asks of us.

272
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:29,720
He wants the Holy Spirit to produce this type of patience in our life that extends grace to the most difficult people.

273
00:20:29,720 --> 00:20:35,980
In the worst situations, I think the application question we can ask for ourselves in this section is,

274
00:20:35,980 --> 00:20:41,080
how does God's level of patience towards you impact your level of patience towards other people?

275
00:20:41,080 --> 00:20:47,500
Are you receiving the fullness of the patience of God?

276
00:20:47,500 --> 00:20:55,060
Are you understanding how much rebellion that God has overcome through his patience to bring about just to get us Jesus?

277
00:20:55,060 --> 00:21:02,580
Like that in and of itself, before he shows any patience to you individually for all of your sins and mess ups and failures and brokenness,

278
00:21:02,580 --> 00:21:07,620
like just the amount of patience he had with the nation of Israel to deliver Jesus to us.

279
00:21:07,620 --> 00:21:10,540
He was almost done with them at Moses' time.

280
00:21:10,540 --> 00:21:12,960
They make the golden calf, right?

281
00:21:13,360 --> 00:21:17,480
They're like, as soon as God brings them out of Egypt, they're like, we don't like this food that he's giving us.

282
00:21:17,480 --> 00:21:19,900
We'd rather have the watermelons that we had when we were in slavery.

283
00:21:19,900 --> 00:21:21,460
So let's just go back to slavery.

284
00:21:21,460 --> 00:21:24,260
And God's like, cool, awesome.

285
00:21:24,260 --> 00:21:26,100
Well, Moses, come up here.

286
00:21:26,100 --> 00:21:26,840
I'll give you the law.

287
00:21:26,840 --> 00:21:28,620
And then maybe the people will change their mind.

288
00:21:28,620 --> 00:21:32,020
And before Moses even gets down the mountain, they've already made a golden calf,

289
00:21:32,020 --> 00:21:34,540
and they're bowing down and worshiping a cow.

290
00:21:34,540 --> 00:21:37,200
And God's like, all right, I'm done.

291
00:21:37,200 --> 00:21:39,320
You guys can go to the promised land without me.

292
00:21:41,320 --> 00:21:44,180
Moses says that you said you're patient.

293
00:21:44,180 --> 00:21:48,440
You said you're slow to anger, bounding and steadfast love.

294
00:21:48,440 --> 00:21:50,620
And God's like, oh, that's right.

295
00:21:50,620 --> 00:21:51,300
I did say that.

296
00:21:51,300 --> 00:21:52,960
I did say that.

297
00:21:52,960 --> 00:21:54,900
And I am, and I will be patient with you.

298
00:21:54,900 --> 00:21:57,640
How patient God was with the nation of Israel.

299
00:21:57,640 --> 00:22:02,280
And all of that patience was to bring us Jesus at the right time, in the right place,

300
00:22:02,280 --> 00:22:04,920
so that we might receive salvation.

301
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:11,080
How does God's level of patience towards us impact our level of patience towards one another?

302
00:22:11,080 --> 00:22:15,680
That's a tough question that I have been wrestling with ever since yesterday morning.

303
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:19,300
Let's continue to wrestle with that thought.

304
00:22:19,300 --> 00:22:25,780
And unfortunately, if you found this aspect of patience difficult, it only gets worse.

305
00:22:25,780 --> 00:22:28,900
Because people can be avoided, right?

306
00:22:28,900 --> 00:22:30,940
Like difficult people, we can be avoided.

307
00:22:31,140 --> 00:22:35,840
We can avoid difficult people, but oftentimes we can't get out of difficult circumstances.

308
00:22:35,840 --> 00:22:39,120
And that's the second aspect of Christian patience.

309
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:45,480
Christian patience is not only about extending grace to difficult people, but it's about enduring difficult circumstances.

310
00:22:45,480 --> 00:22:49,100
Look what James says as he's giving this diatribe on patience.

311
00:22:49,100 --> 00:22:57,940
He says in verse 10, for example, for examples of patience in suffering, look at the prophets who spoke in the name of the Lord.

312
00:22:57,940 --> 00:23:03,200
We give honor to those who endure patiently under suffering.

313
00:23:03,200 --> 00:23:07,280
For instance, you know about Job, a man of great endurance.

314
00:23:07,280 --> 00:23:14,220
You can see how the Lord was kind to him at the end, for the Lord is full of tenderness and mercy.

315
00:23:15,560 --> 00:23:24,480
So not only does James say patience is about not grumbling with difficult people, extending grace to difficult people, but it's about enduring difficult circumstances.

316
00:23:24,480 --> 00:23:26,280
And he gives two examples.

317
00:23:26,280 --> 00:23:27,260
First, the prophets.

318
00:23:27,260 --> 00:23:36,300
I was reading something a couple weeks ago, and they said, do you realize that like 99% of the Scriptures were written by people who were suffering?

319
00:23:36,300 --> 00:23:39,320
The prophets were suffering.

320
00:23:39,320 --> 00:23:44,020
Like God told them to go tell their people all of the judgment that they were going to have.

321
00:23:44,020 --> 00:23:46,980
Most of the prophets were rejected by their people.

322
00:23:46,980 --> 00:23:49,520
Nobody wanted to listen to the sermons that they were preaching.

323
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:51,580
They were in suffering and difficulty.

324
00:23:51,580 --> 00:23:56,780
And the 1% of the Bible that was written by somebody who wasn't suffering is the most depressing book in all of Scripture.

325
00:23:56,780 --> 00:23:57,860
It's Ecclesiastes.

326
00:23:57,860 --> 00:24:03,540
Solomon had all of the money, all of the wealth, all of the resources, and he writes the depressing book.

327
00:24:03,540 --> 00:24:06,940
Because he wrote it from an earthly perspective.

328
00:24:06,940 --> 00:24:09,660
Meanwhile, the prophets, they have hope for the future.

329
00:24:09,660 --> 00:24:16,840
They know who God is and what he's like, and so they can endure suffering because they know there's a hope that's coming on the other side.

330
00:24:16,840 --> 00:24:24,660
Meanwhile, Solomon, who doesn't have to endure suffering, rather he gets to enjoy the beauty and all of the good gifts of God.

331
00:24:24,740 --> 00:24:36,560
He's depressed in the middle of all of it because he says, if this is all there is, if all I get is sex, money, drugs, rock and roll, if this is all I get, this stinks.

332
00:24:36,560 --> 00:24:38,720
He says, woe is me, right?

333
00:24:38,720 --> 00:24:42,740
Like, I'm just like, I'm just going to eat, drink, and have a good time, and then die.

334
00:24:42,740 --> 00:24:46,720
And I'll go back to dust, and the earth will swallow me up.

335
00:24:46,800 --> 00:24:48,480
It's a really encouraging book of the Bible.

336
00:24:48,480 --> 00:24:49,960
I read it this week.

337
00:24:49,960 --> 00:25:06,940
But the prophets who are in the midst of suffering, the opposite experience of Solomon, endure patiently because they know who God is, and they know what God's promised to do.

338
00:25:07,980 --> 00:25:09,720
The same is true for Job.

339
00:25:09,720 --> 00:25:12,940
Those of you who grew up in Sunday school, you've heard the story of Job.

340
00:25:12,940 --> 00:25:21,140
Job had everything, and then everything was taken away from him with no explanation, no warning, no understanding of anything.

341
00:25:21,140 --> 00:25:26,880
And yet, through the most difficult circumstances, Job patiently endured.

342
00:25:26,880 --> 00:25:27,220
Why?

343
00:25:27,220 --> 00:25:29,420
Because he knew God.

344
00:25:29,420 --> 00:25:31,560
He knew God.

345
00:25:31,560 --> 00:25:32,560
He knew who God is.

346
00:25:32,560 --> 00:25:33,360
He knew what God is like.

347
00:25:33,360 --> 00:25:37,380
He knows that God is a patient God, slow to anger, abounding in love.

348
00:25:37,380 --> 00:25:40,580
He knows the promises that God has made for the future.

349
00:25:40,580 --> 00:25:55,240
And so there's a distinct difference between the type of patience that somebody has who knows God, who's experienced the great patience of God, who knows the promise of the future.

350
00:25:55,240 --> 00:26:07,220
There's a difference in the type of patience that we can carry through difficult circumstances if we're confident in who God is and what he's done and what he promised to do versus the person who's not.

351
00:26:07,380 --> 00:26:08,440
We're not really quite sure.

352
00:26:08,440 --> 00:26:13,560
Do you see this in the difference between Job's response and Job's wife's response?

353
00:26:13,560 --> 00:26:17,560
So Job loses everything.

354
00:26:17,560 --> 00:26:19,820
God takes everything away from him.

355
00:26:19,820 --> 00:26:23,200
All his kids, all of his wealth, everything.

356
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:24,040
His health.

357
00:26:24,040 --> 00:26:28,520
Job stood up and he tore his robe in grief.

358
00:26:28,520 --> 00:26:31,500
He's not ignoring pain or sadness or difficulties.

359
00:26:31,500 --> 00:26:32,900
He's feeling it.

360
00:26:33,380 --> 00:26:34,780
He shaved his head.

361
00:26:34,780 --> 00:26:36,700
He fell to the ground and he worshipped.

362
00:26:36,700 --> 00:26:38,660
And he said this,

363
00:26:38,660 --> 00:26:43,180
I came naked from my mother's womb and I will be naked when I leave.

364
00:26:43,180 --> 00:26:46,800
The Lord gave me what I had and the Lord has taken it away.

365
00:26:46,800 --> 00:26:49,060
Praise the name of the Lord.

366
00:26:50,340 --> 00:26:58,480
That response comes from a guy who knows God, knows what he's like and knows what he's promised to do.

367
00:26:58,480 --> 00:27:05,420
Versus Job's wife who's wrestling, who's struggling, who's not sure if she quite knows God or believes what he says.

368
00:27:05,420 --> 00:27:09,520
He says this, Job scraped his skin with a piece of broken pottery.

369
00:27:09,520 --> 00:27:12,820
I thought about taking that out, but I figured it would be a nice comedic release here.

370
00:27:12,820 --> 00:27:14,860
Job was just like so struggling.

371
00:27:14,860 --> 00:27:20,100
He's scraping his skin with a piece of broken pottery as he sat among the ashes and his wife said to him,

372
00:27:20,100 --> 00:27:23,320
Are you still trying to maintain your integrity?

373
00:27:23,320 --> 00:27:25,800
Curse God and die.

374
00:27:25,800 --> 00:27:34,000
This is the difference between somebody who's confident in who God is and what he's done and what he's promised to do

375
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,320
versus somebody who thinks this is all there is.

376
00:27:36,320 --> 00:27:43,020
What we had, the stuff that we had, the wealth and the kids and everything, that's all there was and now it's gone.

377
00:27:43,020 --> 00:27:44,560
So what is God going to do for us?

378
00:27:44,560 --> 00:27:53,000
The ability to patiently endure suffering that the prophets and that Job had came from their understanding of who God is,

379
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:56,960
that he's patient and their confidence in what he's promised to do,

380
00:27:56,960 --> 00:28:01,960
that he is coming back to restore the difficult circumstances of this life.

381
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:15,100
So, Christian, follower of Jesus, you and I, we live in a broken world, destined for restoration but not yet there.

382
00:28:15,100 --> 00:28:19,560
We will experience tremendous pain and difficulty.

383
00:28:19,560 --> 00:28:24,280
We will interact with extremely difficult people.

384
00:28:27,960 --> 00:28:33,540
However, the Holy Spirit can produce patience in us.

385
00:28:33,540 --> 00:28:40,880
Patience through difficult circumstances and patience with difficult people because we know God.

386
00:28:40,880 --> 00:28:42,600
We know who he is.

387
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:43,980
He's been patient to us.

388
00:28:43,980 --> 00:28:46,220
He's been sending grace to us.

389
00:28:46,220 --> 00:28:53,760
And Jesus, our perfect example, our great high priest, endured the most difficult of circumstances for you and for I.

390
00:28:53,760 --> 00:28:59,980
To give us hope and a future so that we can endure difficult circumstances.

391
00:28:59,980 --> 00:29:07,440
If you're in here and you're not really sure where you're at with Jesus or if you're a follower of Jesus,

392
00:29:07,440 --> 00:29:14,980
maybe you're in that spot where Job's wife was, wrestling, dealing with difficult people and difficult circumstances,

393
00:29:14,980 --> 00:29:18,500
not really sure if God is who he says he is.

394
00:29:19,960 --> 00:29:29,180
Extending grace to difficult people and enduring difficult circumstances for you becomes a lot more doable if you acknowledge the patience of God in your life.

395
00:29:29,180 --> 00:29:33,740
If you receive God's patience towards you, his kindness towards you,

396
00:29:33,740 --> 00:29:41,360
and if you accept by faith the future promise that he is secured through you by the death, burial, and resurrection of Jesus Christ.

397
00:29:43,500 --> 00:29:45,140
It's a decision away.

398
00:29:45,140 --> 00:29:48,080
Patience is a decision away.

399
00:29:48,080 --> 00:29:56,020
Receive the Lord's patience and kindness to you because at some point it ends.

400
00:29:56,020 --> 00:30:04,960
See, for you good Bible scholars out here, you might have realized that I left off half of Exodus 34 when I read it.

401
00:30:06,760 --> 00:30:08,220
There's a little bit left.

402
00:30:08,220 --> 00:30:10,180
It says this, Exodus 34, 67,

403
00:30:10,180 --> 00:30:15,440
The Lord, the Lord, compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in love and faithfulness,

404
00:30:15,440 --> 00:30:19,880
maintaining love to the thousands, and forgiving wickedness, rebellion, and sin.

405
00:30:19,880 --> 00:30:24,560
But I do not excuse the guilty.

406
00:30:24,560 --> 00:30:28,720
I lay the sins of the parents upon their children and their grandchildren.

407
00:30:28,720 --> 00:30:34,260
The entire family is affected, even children in the third and fourth generations.

408
00:30:34,260 --> 00:30:41,620
God is a patient God who constantly begs us to come into his presence and to receive his patience.

409
00:30:41,620 --> 00:30:46,320
But for those of us that refuse, and refuse, and refuse, and refuse,

410
00:30:46,320 --> 00:30:49,720
one day we will feel the weight of the judgment of God,

411
00:30:49,720 --> 00:30:54,920
not because he wasn't patient with us, but because we rejected that patience.

412
00:30:54,920 --> 00:31:00,460
So if you're here and you haven't received the patience of God in your life

413
00:31:00,460 --> 00:31:04,660
through the death, burial, and resurrection of Jesus, what are you waiting on?

414
00:31:04,660 --> 00:31:09,260
God is extending that patience to you, that forgiveness to you,

415
00:31:09,260 --> 00:31:13,300
that promise of a future hope for you, that thing that Dave talked about this morning.

416
00:31:13,300 --> 00:31:17,680
You guys look at fireworks and we get excited about the explosions and the celebration.

417
00:31:17,680 --> 00:31:21,500
It's going to be far, far greater when Christ Jesus returns,

418
00:31:21,500 --> 00:31:25,220
riding on the clouds, coming to wipe away every tear from our eyes.

419
00:31:25,900 --> 00:31:29,220
There will be no more mourning, no more pain, no more sickness.

420
00:31:29,220 --> 00:31:31,580
Here he is helping me, please.

421
00:31:31,580 --> 00:31:36,320
She found Revelation 1, verse 7, so at least she's got good theology.

422
00:31:38,120 --> 00:31:41,280
So, if that's you, if you're here and you're not sure where you're at with Jesus,

423
00:31:41,280 --> 00:31:44,880
I'd encourage you to talk to me, talk to Dave, talk to one of our leaders,

424
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:46,760
or your friend that you came with.

425
00:31:46,760 --> 00:31:54,800
And for all of us, patience is not going to come by me giving you a couple practical tips

426
00:31:54,800 --> 00:31:58,020
of how to be more emotionally healthy or present.

427
00:31:58,020 --> 00:31:59,500
Those things are good and fine.

428
00:31:59,500 --> 00:32:04,760
But the patience that Paul wants for us, the patience that Paul describes in the fruit of the Spirit,

429
00:32:04,760 --> 00:32:09,040
is the perfect patience of Christ, and no practical tip is going to get that for you.

430
00:32:09,040 --> 00:32:11,900
You've got to spend time with the Lord.

431
00:32:11,900 --> 00:32:16,980
You've got to allow the Holy Spirit to produce that type of fruit in you.

432
00:32:16,980 --> 00:32:20,380
It won't come through implementing a few practical strategies,

433
00:32:20,380 --> 00:32:24,300
but rather through knowing Jesus and seeking to become more like him.

434
00:32:26,500 --> 00:32:31,400
So, we're going to transition here in a few minutes to a time of communion.

435
00:32:31,400 --> 00:32:36,900
And as we do that, I really thought the best way for us to end our time this morning

436
00:32:36,900 --> 00:32:40,560
would be to meditate on a passage of Scripture that I think summarizes perfectly

437
00:32:40,560 --> 00:32:45,140
both of these principles of patience in the life of Jesus.

438
00:32:46,540 --> 00:32:51,800
That Jesus is the source of true, genuine patience.

439
00:32:51,800 --> 00:32:56,100
That Jesus perfectly extends grace to difficult people.

440
00:32:56,100 --> 00:33:02,240
And that Jesus perfectly endures difficult circumstances at the end of his life.

441
00:33:02,240 --> 00:33:04,720
We read this in Luke.

442
00:33:05,620 --> 00:33:09,440
Then the entire council took Jesus to Pilate, the Roman governor,

443
00:33:09,440 --> 00:33:11,920
and they began to state their case.

444
00:33:11,920 --> 00:33:17,820
This man has been leading our people astray by telling them not to pay their taxes to the Roman government.

445
00:33:17,820 --> 00:33:18,560
Not true.

446
00:33:18,560 --> 00:33:22,020
And by claiming that he is the Messiah, a king.

447
00:33:22,020 --> 00:33:22,800
True.

448
00:33:24,040 --> 00:33:25,360
So, Pilate asked him,

449
00:33:25,360 --> 00:33:26,900
Are you the king of the Jews?

450
00:33:26,900 --> 00:33:27,720
Jesus replied,

451
00:33:27,720 --> 00:33:28,920
You have said it.

452
00:33:28,920 --> 00:33:32,480
Pilate turned to the leading priests and to the crowd and said,

453
00:33:32,480 --> 00:33:34,780
I find nothing wrong with this man.

454
00:33:34,780 --> 00:33:35,480
He's innocent.

455
00:33:35,480 --> 00:33:40,940
But his people, the people that were supposed to be his family,

456
00:33:40,940 --> 00:33:43,380
they became insistent.

457
00:33:43,380 --> 00:33:47,100
He is causing riots by his teaching wherever he goes,

458
00:33:47,100 --> 00:33:49,440
all over Judea, from Galilee into Jerusalem.

459
00:33:50,860 --> 00:33:57,640
Meanwhile, the leading priests and the teachers of the religious law stood there shouting their accusations.

460
00:33:57,640 --> 00:34:02,960
Then Herod and his soldiers began mocking and ridiculing Jesus' difficult people.

461
00:34:02,960 --> 00:34:12,160
Finally, they put a royal robe on him and sent him back to Pilate.

462
00:34:12,160 --> 00:34:17,140
Then Pilate called together his leading priests and other religious leaders along with the people.

463
00:34:17,140 --> 00:34:20,460
He announced his verdict.

464
00:34:20,860 --> 00:34:24,320
You brought this man to me, accusing him of leading a revolt.

465
00:34:24,320 --> 00:34:29,360
I have examined him thoroughly on this point in your presence,

466
00:34:29,360 --> 00:34:31,560
and I find him innocent.

467
00:34:31,560 --> 00:34:36,720
Herod came to the same conclusion, and they sent him back to us.

468
00:34:36,720 --> 00:34:40,340
Nothing this man has done calls for the death penalty.

469
00:34:40,340 --> 00:34:43,960
So I will have him flogged, and I will release him.

470
00:34:44,080 --> 00:34:48,460
Then a roar rose from the crowd, and with one voice they shouted,

471
00:34:48,460 --> 00:34:51,700
Kill him and release Barabbas.

472
00:34:51,700 --> 00:34:54,300
Difficult people.

473
00:34:54,300 --> 00:35:04,520
Barabbas was a prisoner who had taken part in an insurrection against the government and had murdered people.

474
00:35:05,740 --> 00:35:10,200
Meanwhile, Jesus was innocent, and Pilate argued with them because he wants to release Jesus.

475
00:35:10,200 --> 00:35:12,500
But they kept on shouting, Crucify him!

476
00:35:12,500 --> 00:35:13,460
Crucify him!

477
00:35:13,460 --> 00:35:14,540
Difficult people.

478
00:35:14,540 --> 00:35:18,320
For the third time, he demanded, Why?

479
00:35:18,320 --> 00:35:19,940
What crime has he committed?

480
00:35:19,940 --> 00:35:23,040
I have found no reason to sentence him to death.

481
00:35:23,040 --> 00:35:25,960
So I will have him flogged, and then I will release him.

482
00:35:26,000 --> 00:35:30,080
But the mob shouted louder and louder, demanding that Jesus be crucified.

483
00:35:30,080 --> 00:35:32,440
And their voices prevailed.

484
00:35:32,440 --> 00:35:36,280
Difficult people.

485
00:35:36,280 --> 00:35:43,340
Two others, both criminals, were let out to be executed with him.

486
00:35:43,340 --> 00:35:49,660
And when they came to the place called the skull, they nailed him to the cross.

487
00:35:49,660 --> 00:35:52,400
Difficult circumstances.

488
00:35:55,400 --> 00:36:01,200
The criminals were there also crucified, one on his right and one on his left.

489
00:36:01,200 --> 00:36:10,200
Jesus, in the midst of the most difficult circumstances,

490
00:36:10,200 --> 00:36:15,920
surrounded by some of the most difficult people that you could ever imagine,

491
00:36:15,920 --> 00:36:21,800
sets this, with patience.

492
00:36:23,800 --> 00:36:28,240
Forgive them, for they don't know what they're doing.

493
00:36:28,240 --> 00:36:31,640
This is our God.

494
00:36:31,640 --> 00:36:35,380
Patiently enduring difficult circumstances.

495
00:36:35,380 --> 00:36:38,720
And patiently extending grace to difficult people.

496
00:36:42,220 --> 00:36:43,260
Do you want to have patience?

497
00:36:43,260 --> 00:36:43,440
Do you want to have patience?

498
00:36:43,440 --> 00:36:43,460
Do you want to have patience?

499
00:36:48,580 --> 00:36:53,180
Read about the promises of Jesus for your life.

500
00:36:53,180 --> 00:36:54,280
Do you want to have patience?

501
00:36:54,280 --> 00:37:00,860
Remember how patient God has been to you.

502
00:37:03,020 --> 00:37:09,780
How patient he's been to your friends and family as he awaits his return while he will restore everything to his rightful place.

503
00:37:09,820 --> 00:37:10,460
Let's pray.

504
00:37:10,460 --> 00:37:10,620
Let's pray.

505
00:37:10,620 --> 00:37:16,060
Father, we love you and we thank you for your patience and for your grace to us.

506
00:37:18,860 --> 00:37:30,120
God, we want to be a people that manifest that same type of patience to the difficult people in our life that endure with patience the difficult circumstances that we are faced with.

507
00:37:30,120 --> 00:37:41,480
Not because we have some inner strength, not because we have some practical tips that are going to get us through difficult circumstances, but because we know you.

508
00:37:41,480 --> 00:37:47,720
We're confident in your patience towards us and we're confident in your promise for the future.

509
00:37:47,880 --> 00:37:53,180
And so, we patiently endure difficult circumstances.

510
00:37:53,180 --> 00:37:57,160
We patiently extend grace to difficult people.

511
00:37:57,160 --> 00:38:12,560
All with the hope that more people would receive your patience, that more people would come into the family of God, sit at the table of the kingdom of God for your glory and for our good.

512
00:38:12,560 --> 00:38:14,200
In Jesus' name I pray.

513
00:38:14,200 --> 00:38:14,900
Amen.

514
00:38:17,880 --> 00:38:18,880
Amen.
